<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="ARM:LE:32:v7:default">
<!--
    calltargettype (GH-869) on ARMv7 hard-float: the function-pointer type built for an
    indirect call agrees with where the call passes its values, or the call
    keeps void *.

    Stage: P5 type recovery, after every variable has its final type and just
    before casts are decided. P0 assertion: option calltargettype on|off.

    Source (clang 14 -target armv7-linux-gnueabihf -O2 -fno-pic, the object's .text at 0x10000, its
    relocations left unapplied so the tables read from the image):

        typedef float (*f1)(float);
        typedef int (*i2)(int, int);
        extern f1 ftab[];
        extern i2 itab[];
        void fstore(unsigned i, float *p) { p[0] = ftab[i & 1](p[1]); }
        float fmul(unsigned i, float x) { return ftab[i & 1](x * 2.0f) + 1.0f; }
        int icall(unsigned i, int a, int b) { return itab[i & 1](a, b) + 3; }

    Pass 1 (ctypes on, calltargettype off) is the bug: every target prints
    void *. Pass 2 (calltargettype on) types fmul's target float (*)(float),
    the float passed and returned in s0 (asserts 1, 2), and icall's int one
    (assert 3). fstore passes the float it loaded, which recovery holds as an
    unsigned int in s0; a type built from that unsigned int would pass it in
    r0/w0, so the call keeps void * in both passes (asserts 4 to 6).

    Assertions span the combined output of both passes.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x10000" readonly="true">
